= Preface

TK
Most of the chapters in this document were written in preparation for lesson-blocks taught at _Klingenspiel_#index[Klingenspiel] in Vienna.
They are part handouts, part documentation, and part a way for me to work through a topic.
Each time I teach a topic, I update the chapter with new drills, ideas, and notes.

Who is it for

Style of fencing

What I am looking for?

I am looking for a style#index[Style] of rapier fencing which
- is at least as 'martial' as it is 'art'.
- is based on a set of principles that simultaneously can be derived from the treatises and are logically sound.
- has viable defensive and offensive options
- treats the rapier as a 'thrust-and-cut' weapon the same ways as a sabre is a 'cut-and-thrust' weapon.
- integrates footwork, bladework, and body movement
- considers the empty hand (the 'other strong hand') as an integral component of every action.
- is not linear.
- not averse to using all parts of the rapier in offence and defence (pommel, quillons, etc.).
- is distinctly rapier, not 'heavy foil' or 'long sabre'.
